#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100667 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43f839b4-2b0e-5ab5-809a-741747f1016d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Power and Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "25e1588c-860d-5285-8616-219770b157e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-650" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac{n^{100}}{e^n}$ for $n\\geq 1$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n$, we have\n\n$$\nn! \\gg e^{n} \\gg n^m \\gg \\ln(n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gg$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m \\gt 0$.\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty} a_n = \\lim_{n\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{n^{100}}{e^n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to\\infty$. However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{n^{100}}{e^n} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100668 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e00ff7-a91e-5b56-9f3a-24e7318cf004" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence Sequences Containing Power and Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "bd5a2aa1-030d-5dc6-94cb-8482148e9888" :question/math-academy-id "e-651" :question/problem "Does the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac{n}{\\ln{n}}$ for $n\\gt 1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n$, we have\n\n$$\nn! \\gg e^{n} \\gg n^m \\gg \\ln(n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gg$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m \\gt 0$.\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty} a_n = \\lim_{n\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{n}{\\ln{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to\\infty$. However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{n}{\\ln{n}} = \\infty\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist. Therefore, the sequence diverges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100669 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51d50237-f6cf-5c2d-b8d1-335378205a28" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Exponential Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5ea18071-098a-5514-8a15-d2478bbc68c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-652" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac{e^{2n}}{\\ln{n}}$ for $n\\geq 1$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n$, we have\n\n$$\nn! \\gg e^{n} \\gg n^m \\gg \\ln(n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\gg$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m \\gt 0$.\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty} a_n = \\lim_{n\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{e^{2n}}{\\ln{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $x\\to\\infty$. However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{e^{2n}}{\\ln{n}} = \\infty\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist. Therefore, the sequence diverges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43f839b4-2b0e-5ab5-809a-741747f1016d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Power and Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ec650b-c3d8-50e2-8019-b3f5db04dcb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099781} {:answer/id #uuid "d572bdd8-639e-56ee-a240-df640b79094d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099782} {:answer/id #uuid "f289bf3e-d0d1-5204-a976-1e479fa80121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099783} {:answer/id #uuid "1acc63fa-11e8-574d-a249-8b2df347b72e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099784} {:answer/id #uuid "3e42c4d6-83a6-51cc-88ec-8f7386ab32f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e42c4d6-83a6-51cc-88ec-8f7386ab32f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099785} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e14d885-1c74-5f30-981b-55fa9b0375d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099780}] :question/id #uuid "c887b91e-985b-5a9c-b288-e71bb3c8cdca" :question/math-academy-id "q-49670" :question/problem "Does the sequence $a_{n} = \\frac{e^{n}}{n}$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?"}]}
